@prefix atlas:   <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/> .
@prefix prim:    <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/> .
@prefix obj:     <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/object/> .
@prefix cco:     <https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/> .
@prefix obo:     <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/atlas-core>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:versionIRI <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/2026-08-01/atlas-core> ;
    owl:imports <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/imports/cco-bfo-reference> ;
    rdfs:label "Definition Atlas — Core"@en ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment """Scaffold for a catalogue of formal mathematical definitions of "system".

WHAT THIS ONTOLOGY ASSERTS: that certain DEFINITIONS exist, are borne by certain
documents, and invoke certain terms.

WHAT IT DOES NOT ASSERT: that the systems those definitions purport to define
exist. CCO's `is about` (cco:ont00001808) is deliberately left unasserted on
entries. This keeps the catalogue neutral between realist traditions (Bunge) and
constructivist ones (Klir), which is the whole point of the exercise: a merged or
realism-committed vocabulary would erase the very disagreements being catalogued.

WHY CCO: a definition is a generically dependent continuant that is about
something — exactly what BFO's GDC category and CCO's Information Content Entity
were introduced for. Alignment is therefore at the ANNOTATION layer only. The
per-tradition vocabularies (Klir's, Bunge's, Mobus's...) are the CONTENT of these
ICEs and are NOT re-parented as BFO domain ontologies.

TIER: this file supports tier 0 (bibliographic + verbatim + primitives invoked).
Tier 1 (dependency quiver) and above are not yet modelled here.

Spec: strategy/phd/definition-atlas-spec.md in the Halcyonic vault."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Classes
##############################################################################

atlas:FormalSystemDefinition
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cco:ont00000853 ;   # Descriptive Information Content Entity
    rdfs:label "Formal System Definition"@en ;
    skos:definition """A Descriptive Information Content Entity that defines the
term "system" formally: it introduces named components with types or sorts, and
fixes at least one relation, function, or constraint among them, in symbols or in
prose precise enough to be transcribed to symbols without adding content."""@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Inclusion criteria are stated in the spec, §5. Borderline cases are adjudicated there, not here."@en .


atlas:Example
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Example"@en ;
    skos:definition """A case an author rules on: something they offer as a system, or refuse as
one. Reified rather than recorded as a string on the entry, because an example is a thing
several claims are made about -- where it appears in the source, how the encoding was
established, and which shared case it instantiates."""@en ;
    rdfs:comment """WHY THIS IS A CLASS. Examples were literal strings on the entry until
2026-08-03, which made four things impossible at once: an example could carry no evidence code
of its own, so one entry-level code graded a verbatim, five primitives and three examples
alike; no source location, though Bunge's examples sit outside the location his entry claims;
no separation of the case from the author's reason for it, concatenated with an em dash; and
no identity, so two authors ruling on the same object could not be matched. See
docs/proposals/P4-evidence-scope.md."""@en .

atlas:RetiredTerm
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Retired Term"@en ;
    skos:definition """A tombstone: an IRI this catalogue once served as a live term and will
never serve as one again. IRIs are permanent (w3id) — a term is never deleted and never
silently mutated; it is retired by CHANGING ITS CLASS to this one, so the strict shapes for
live terms keep their teeth and a stale citation still resolves to a signpost. A retired term
carries owl:deprecated true, at least one dcterms:isReplacedBy successor, and a comment saying
why. Policy and procedure: docs/iri-policy.md (D5). The reader's build refuses to serve a
deprecated IRI as live data, and that refusal is gated."""@en .

atlas:TestObject
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Test Object"@en ;
    skos:definition """An object that definitions rule on, named once so that two authors ruling
on it can be identified as ruling on the SAME thing. Klir's "a collection of books ordered by
authors' names" and Bunge's "a collection of events, even if ordered" are two cases of one test
object; no string comparison will ever discover that."""@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A TEST OBJECT IS ITSELF A CLAIM, and carries its own evidence code for that
reason. Asserting that two cases are about one object is an interpretation of both, made by
whoever draws the vocabulary — not something read off either source. Grading the object rather
than each link avoids a regress: the link inherits the identification's grade, and the
identification is argued in the mapping layer via atlas:arguedIn.

EXTERNAL AND PRIOR, like D1's candidate fix for the primitive scheme. A test object must be a
case an author actually ruled on, never one invented to make a comparison come out. If nothing
in the corpus rules on it, it does not belong here."""@en .

atlas:EvidenceCode
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence Code"@en ;
    skos:definition "How an encoding was established. Modelled on the Gene Ontology's evidence codes: every assertion carries a record of its provenance strength, and unverified assertions remain visible and filterable rather than silently equal to verified ones."@en .


##############################################################################
# Object properties
##############################################################################

atlas:invokesPrimitive
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:range skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "invokes primitive"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a definition to a term it uses as a primitive. The census payload: which primitives recur across definitions, and which are unique to one."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Range is skos:Concept, not an OWL class, deliberately. A primitive here is a TERM USED IN A DEFINITION, not a kind of thing in the world. SKOS is about concepts; using it avoids smuggling realism into constructivist traditions."@en .

atlas:admits
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:range atlas:Example ;
    rdfs:label "admits"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a definition to a case its author offers as a system."@en .

atlas:refuses
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:range atlas:Example ;
    rdfs:label "refuses"@en ;
    skos:definition """Relates a definition to a case its author says is NOT a system on it.
The separating-instance data: where two definitions rule differently on the same case is where
the loss catalogue comes from."""@en ;
    rdfs:comment """NOT EVERY NEGATIVE IS A REFUSAL. Bunge refuses ontically -- no bonds,
therefore not a system, and no act of description alters it. Klir's "a collection of books is
not a system, only a set" is not a refusal at all: on his criterion an object is a system iff
it CAN BE DESCRIBED as conforming, and a bare collection can be. Recording the second as a
refusal would manufacture a symmetry the authors do not share. See
docs/proposals/P3-stance-axes.md."""@en .

atlas:instantiates
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain atlas:Example ;
    rdfs:range atlas:TestObject ;
    rdfs:label "instantiates"@en ;
    skos:definition """Relates a case to the test object it is a case of. This is what makes a
disagreement derivable: same test object, one definition admitting and another refusing, is a
separating instance the build can find rather than one an encoder has to assert."""@en .

atlas:arguedIn
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "argued in"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where the claim carried by this term is argued — normally a document in mappings/, which owes a witness and must name its presentation."@en .

atlas:statedIn
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:range cco:ont00000253 ;   # Information Bearing Entity
    rdfs:label "stated in"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relates a definition to the document that carries it."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "TODO: align to the inverse of obo:BFO_0000101 (carrier of) once the CCO carrier chain has been read in full. Declared standalone for now rather than asserted wrongly."@en .


##############################################################################
# Annotation properties — provenance and evidence
##############################################################################

atlas:verbatim
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "verbatim"@en ;
    skos:definition "The definition exactly as written in the source, transcribed without paraphrase. The thing every downstream encoding must be checkable against."@en .

atlas:sourceLocation
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "source location"@en ;
    skos:definition "Where in the bearer the verbatim text appears — equation number, page, section."@en .

atlas:displayForm
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "display form"@en ;
    skos:definition """The span of the verbatim that is the definition's formal statement — the
face a reader meets first, e.g. `S = (T, R)` or `σ = ⟨C, E, S⟩`. Presentation only: it adds no
content, it marks a span. MUST be an exact substring of atlas:verbatim on the same individual —
the build refuses an entry where it is not, so this property can never put words in an author's
mouth. Absent where the definition has no formal statement to lift; the verbatim then faces
whole."""@en .

atlas:displayContext
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "display context"@en ;
    skos:definition """The span of the verbatim that is the author's own plain-language reading
of the display form — Klir's "where S, T, R denote…", Bunge's disjointness clause. Same contract
as atlas:displayForm: an exact substring of atlas:verbatim, enforced by the build. Together the
two spans let an interface lead with the mathematics and follow with the author's gloss without
one editorial word being added."""@en .

atlas:evidenceCode
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "evidence code"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which atlas:EvidenceCode individual applies to this encoding."@en .

atlas:authorCaveat
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "author caveat"@en ;
    skos:definition "A qualification the AUTHOR places on their own definition — that it is provisional, not a definition proper, too general, or holds only under stated conditions. Recorded verbatim. Suppressing these is how a catalogue starts misrepresenting its sources."@en .

atlas:gloss
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "gloss"@en ;
    skos:definition """Our description of a case, in our words. Distinct from atlas:verbatim on
the same individual, which is the author's. An example carries both: what the case is, and the
author's own reason for ruling on it as they did."""@en .

atlas:excludedExample
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:label "excluded example"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "DEPRECATED 2026-08-03. Superseded by atlas:refuses onto an atlas:Example. Retained declared so data written against the old shape stays readable."@en ;
    skos:definition "Something the author explicitly says is NOT a system on this definition. The separating-instance data, captured at tier 0: where two definitions disagree about the same object is exactly where the loss catalogue comes from."@en .

atlas:includedExample
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:label "included example"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "DEPRECATED 2026-08-03. Superseded by atlas:admits onto an atlas:Example. Retained declared so data written against the old shape stays readable."@en ;
    skos:definition "Something the author explicitly offers as a system on this definition."@en .

atlas:encodedBy
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "encoded by"@en .

atlas:encodedOn
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "encoded on"@en .

atlas:formalisedAs
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "formalised as"@en ;
    skos:definition """The shape category in systems-science-foundations that encodes this
definition's dependency structure, as a path relative to that repository plus the
declaration name: `Systems/Category/ShapeKlir.lean#KlirShape`."""@en ;
    rdfs:comment """BIBLIOGRAPHIC, NOT A FAITHFULNESS CLAIM. This says only that a
formalisation of this definition exists and where to find it. Whether the quiver
is FAITHFUL to the passage -- which position encodes which primitive, which
phrase licenses which arrow, and what the encoding is silent about -- is a
mapping-layer claim and owes evidence like any other. Recording the pointer here
and the judgement there keeps the two from being confused.

DELIBERATELY A STRING, NOT AN IRI. The foundations are a separate repository with
its own release cadence; a resolvable IRI would pin this catalogue to a commit or
a hosting arrangement neither artifact controls. The reader resolves the path
against a configured checkout and fails the build if the declaration is absent."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Evidence code individuals
##############################################################################

atlas:HVP a atlas:EvidenceCode ;
    rdfs:label "human-verified against primary text"@en ;
    skos:definition "A human read the primary source and confirmed the verbatim text and the encoding against it."@en .

atlas:MDHC a atlas:EvidenceCode ;
    rdfs:label "model-drafted, human-checked"@en ;
    skos:definition "A model produced the encoding; a human checked it against the verbatim text."@en .

atlas:MDU a atlas:EvidenceCode ;
    rdfs:label "model-drafted, unchecked"@en ;
    skos:definition "A model produced the encoding and no human has checked it. MUST NOT be cited in any claim. Present so that coverage figures stay honest."@en .

atlas:PROP a atlas:EvidenceCode ;
    rdfs:label "propagated from another entry"@en ;
    skos:definition "Asserted because a cited source restates another entry's definition. Inherits the strength of its source, never exceeds it."@en .


##############################################################################
# Primitive vocabulary (SKOS) — grows with the census
##############################################################################

atlas:PrimitiveScheme
    a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    rdfs:label "Systems-definition primitive vocabulary"@en ;
    skos:definition "Controlled vocabulary of terms used as primitives in formal definitions of system. Deliberately flat and deliberately not an ontology of the world."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """LEXICAL, NOT SEMANTIC. A primitive here records that an author uses a WORD as
a primitive. Two entries linked to prim:thing are not thereby claimed to mean the same
thing by it — Klir's "thing" is unrestricted, Bunge's ranges over concrete entities in
Theta, and whether those are one primitive or two is a census question this vocabulary
must not prejudge.

The consequence: recurrence counts computed over this scheme are a FIRST-PASS LEXICAL
measure. Semantic co-reference is a mapping-layer claim and owes evidence like any
other. Minting a separate primitive per author would make recurrence trivially zero;
merging them by hand would make it trivially high. Lexical recurrence is the honest
starting point, and its limits are stated here rather than discovered later."""@en .

prim:thing a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "thing"@en .

# Kept distinct from prim:thing on purpose. The scheme is lexical: a primitive
# records that an author uses a WORD, and Bertalanffy says "elements" where Klir
# and Bunge say "things". Collapsing the two would assert a synonymy across
# traditions that this scheme's own scope note denies, and would manufacture a
# shared row the sources do not support.
prim:element a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "element"@en .

prim:relation a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "relation"@en .

prim:set a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "set"@en .

prim:thinghood a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "thinghood"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Klir's term. Recorded as its own primitive rather than folded into `thing`, because whether it is distinct is a census question, not a modelling decision."@en .

prim:systemhood a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "systemhood"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Klir's term, paired with thinghood."@en .

prim:composition a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "composition"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bunge's C. The set of a thing's parts, relativised to a class A and a time t."@en .

prim:environment a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "environment"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bunge's E. Things outside the composition that act on, or are acted on by, components."@en .

prim:structure a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "structure"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bunge's S. Relations among components and between components and environment, partitioned into bonding and nonbonding."@en .

prim:bond a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "bond"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "A bonding relation, as opposed to a merely nonbonding one. Bunge requires the bonding set to be nonempty; this is the primitive that does the refusing."@en .

prim:action a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "action"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bunge's acts-on relation, written x |> y."@en .

prim:part a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "part"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bunge's part-of relation, written x [ y."@en .

prim:time a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "time"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bunge's triple is indexed at a time t. Klir's is not. A candidate point of divergence. In Bertalanffy's eq. (3.1) the invocation is notational — the t of dQᵢ/dt — rather than the word."@en .

prim:measure a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "measure"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bertalanffy's Qᵢ, 'some measure of elements'. The word is his; reading Qᵢ as a state variable is an interpretation and stays out of the scheme."@en .


##############################################################################
# Test objects — grows only when two authors rule on one thing
##############################################################################

atlas:TestObjectScheme
    a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    rdfs:label "Test objects"@en ;
    skos:definition """Objects that definitions of "system" rule on. Deliberately small: a term
is added only when the corpus contains at least two rulings on the same object, because a test
object with one ruling compares nothing and a test object with none is invented."""@en .

obj:ordered-non-bonding
    a atlas:TestObject ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:TestObjectScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "an ordered but unconnected collection"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Members standing in an ordering, with no causal action between them: books
on a shelf by author, events in a sequence.

THE QUALIFIER IS LOAD-BEARING. Bunge refuses this case because ordering is not bonding, not
because the members are events. The refusal runs through his whole apparatus: an aggregate is
a collection "not held together by bonds" (ch. 1 p. 4), Definition 1.1 requires the members
CONNECTED — where a connection, unlike a mere relation "such as that of being older", has at
least one relatum acting on the other (Sec. 1.2) — and Definition 1.2(iii) carries the
nonempty-bondage condition 𝔅_A(σ,t) ≠ ∅ into every system's structure. A shelf of books
ordered by author carries a relation and no bonds. "Unconnected" in the label is Definition
1.1's own word; a plainer "an ordered collection" would understate the disagreement and invite
the reply that he was only excluding events."""@en ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:HVP ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-05"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:arguedIn "mappings/ordered-collection.md" ;
    rdfs:comment """HVP as of 2026-08-05: the identification was model-drafted (2026-08-03) and
has now been human-verified against both primary texts — Bunge's mere-relation/connection
distinction, Definition 1.1, its Example block and Definition 1.2(iii)'s 𝔅≠∅ condition read
against Klir p. 5's criterion and ordering passage. The verifying read confirmed that Klir's
admission is stated as a general principle ("any ordering of a set is a relation defined on
the set") and that Bunge's refusal is bond-absence, not member-kind. The derived conflict
inherits this grade."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Stance — P3 minted, 2026-08-07. Two axes, not one: what an author takes to
# exist is a different commitment from what the observer's act contributes,
# and at least one author (Mobus) holds opposite positions on them. A stance
# attaches to the ENTRY, not the author — a person may write from different
# stances in different works, and "this work is inclined toward" is Mobus's
# own scoping. Assertions are reified individuals (P4, option B): each carries
# its own verbatim, location and evidence code, because an entry-level code
# cannot grade a stance reading separately from a transcription.
#
# Self-ascribed stance lives here, on the entry. One author's characterisation
# of ANOTHER author's stance is a mapping-layer claim and owes a witness; the
# catalogue must be structurally incapable of adopting one author's reading of
# another as fact.
#
# Value concepts enter only with a user, like test objects. The queue is P3's
# table: realist awaits a located Bunge verbatim, selective awaits the Mobus
# entry, formalist awaits Mesarović. Full reasoning and every passage:
# docs/proposals/P3-stance-axes.md.
##############################################################################

atlas:StanceAssertion
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Stance Assertion"@en ;
    skos:definition "A reified reading of what an entry's definition is doing — one value on one axis, carried by the entry, graded by its own evidence code, licensed by its own verbatim."@en .

atlas:hasStance
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has stance"@en ;
    skos:definition "Links an entry to a stance assertion about it. The assertion is about the entry (the work), never the author."@en .

atlas:stanceValue
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "stance value"@en ;
    skos:definition "The value a stance assertion assigns, from one of the two stance schemes. The label is a handle; the assertion's verbatim is the evidence."@en .

atlas:OnticStanceScheme
    a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    rdfs:label "Ontic stance"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the definition takes to exist independently of how anyone knows it. Not a spectrum: perspectivism is a distinct answer, not a midpoint."@en .

atlas:constructivist-about-systemhood a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:OnticStanceScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "constructivist about systemhood"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Systemhood is conferred by an act of distinction or description. Deliberately narrower than 'constructivist': Klir's own clause permits distinctions on real things, which radical constructivism in von Glasersfeld's sense does not."@en .

atlas:perspectivist a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:OnticStanceScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "perspectivist"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bertalanffy's own word (GST ch. 10, 'The Perspectivistic View'). Reality is real, access is perspectival, and the perspective is not free: categories 'must, with a certain tolerance allowed, be isomorphic' to the nexus of real events. Constrained, unlike Nietzschean perspectivism; nearest neighbour is Giere's perspectival realism."@en .

atlas:ObserverRoleScheme
    a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    rdfs:label "The observer's act"@en ;
    skos:definition "What drawing the boundary does, on the entry's own account. NOT a proxy for the ontic axis: Klir's act is constitutive because of his ontology, but Mobus's is selective while his ontology is realist, so the second column cannot be read off the first."@en .

atlas:constitutive a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:ObserverRoleScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "constitutive"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Distinguishing is necessary and sufficient for systemhood. Klir's criterion: an object is a system iff it CAN BE DESCRIBED in a form conforming to eq. (1.1)."@en .


##############################################################################
# Mapping claims — the relation vocabulary, minted 2026-08-07 from the pattern
# across mappings 001–003 rather than ahead of it, as mappings/README.md
# required. Two terms, each with at least two users in prose before it was
# named: weak encompassment (001's Test 1 table; 003's flattening is the same
# instance stated twice) and no-faithful-embedding (001's Joslyn obstruction;
# 003's partition collapse). A claim is a reified individual (P4): the relation
# rides with its evidence code, its argument, and — where one exists — its
# theorem. What the graph can hold is narrower than what the prose holds:
# a claim relates two ENTRIES, so 001's Joslyn⇸Klir obstruction (shape-level,
# no Joslyn entry) and its Myers row (no Myers entry) stay in prose until
# their entries exist. Coverage figures stay honest.
##############################################################################

atlas:MappingClaim
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Mapping Claim"@en ;
    skos:definition "A reified relation between two entries, asserted by a mapping document. Carries its own evidence code and points at the argument; a claim of preservation owes a theorem name, a claim of loss owes a witness."@en .

atlas:claimRelation
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "claim relation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Which relation from the relation scheme this claim asserts between its source and target."@en .

atlas:claimSource
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "claim source"@en ;
    skos:definition "The entry the relation runs from."@en .

atlas:claimTarget
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "claim target"@en ;
    skos:definition "The entry the relation runs to."@en .

atlas:theoremName
    a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "theorem name"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Lean declaration backing this claim, as file#name. Absence on a claim of loss means the theorem is owed, not that none is needed."@en .

atlas:RelationScheme
    a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    rdfs:label "Mapping relations"@en ;
    skos:definition "Relations a mapping claim may assert. A term enters only when at least two prose mappings have needed it; freezing vocabulary ahead of the pattern is the failure this rule prevents."@en .

atlas:weakly-encompasses a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:RelationScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "weakly encompasses"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The source's form can redescribe the target; nothing need be preserved. Vacuously satisfiable by design — mapping 001 shows every catalogued definition passes, and that the constraints distinguishing the target are exactly what redescription is permitted to discard. A claim in this relation is deliberately weak evidence of anything."@en .

atlas:no-faithful-embedding-into a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:RelationScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "admits no faithful embedding into"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "No encoding of the source into the target preserves what the source asserts, using only what the target asserts. The strong reading's failure mode: a claim here owes either a machine-checked obstruction (001: infinite hom-set into finite) or a collapse witness (003: two source systems, one target image)."@en .
